+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+`src/index.ts` holds `resolvePointer` and `JsonPointerError` (RFC 6901). The package is ESM TypeScript (strict) with no runtime dependencies, and tests run with `node --test` on `test/*.test.ts`, importing `../src/index.ts`. Requirements are in specs/json-patch/spec.md; scope is in proposal.md.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:** a small, dependency-free RFC 6902 §4 conformant `applyPatch`, atomic and non-mutating.
+
+**Non-Goals:** diffing, merge patch, JSON text parsing, non-JSON values.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- **Stay in `src/index.ts`**, as in the previous change (re-exports across `.ts` files would need tsconfig changes).
+- **Clone first, mutate the clone.** `applyPatch` deep-clones the document once, applies operations in place on the clone, and returns it. A throw discards the clone, so atomicity (§5) and non-mutation are by construction. Values from `add`/`replace`/`test`-free ops and `copy` sources are deep-cloned when inserted so the result never aliases the patch or itself. Validate every operation's shape before applying any, so a malformed later operation throws before work is done (the result is the same either way, as an error always discards).
+- **Own pointer parsing for write access.** `resolvePointer` only reads, and the writer needs the parent plus last token. A private helper splits and decodes a pointer using the same rules (§3/§4 of RFC 6901: split first, then `~1` before `~0`, reject `~` not followed by 0/1). Parent lookup walks tokens with the same rules as `resolvePointer` (own properties only, array index regex, range check). Duplicating the small decode function is preferable to widening the exported API.
+- **Hostile keys.** Object members are written with `Object.defineProperty` (enumerable, writable, configurable), never `obj[key] = v`, so an own `__proto__` key is created rather than invoking the setter. Cloning uses the same mechanism, so a parsed `__proto__` key survives. Reads use `Object.hasOwn`.
+- **Array indexes.** `add` accepts `-` and indexes `0..length`; other operations require `0..length-1` (`-` refers to no element, RFC 6901 §4, so it errors). Indexes follow `^(0|[1-9][0-9]*)$`, so a leading zero or sign is an error (RFC 6901 §4).
+- **Root cases.** `add`/`replace` at `""` replace the document (§4.1, §4.3). `test` and `copy` from `""` work. `remove` at `""` is unspecified by the RFC (the result would not be a JSON value to return); we throw. `move` from `""` is only possible to a child of itself, which §4.4 forbids, so it throws; `move` from `""` to `""` is a same-location no-op, which we allow like any same-location move.
+- **move.** Check `from` exists, then reject if `from` is a proper prefix of `path` by token comparison (so `/a` vs `/ab` is fine). If `from === path` (same tokens) do nothing. Otherwise remove from `from`, then add at `path` on the post-removal document, as §4.4 says; this is why `/0` to `/2` on a 3-array gives `[b,c,a]`.
+- **replace** on an array assigns in place (same position), which equals remove+add at that index.
+- **test equality** is a private recursive `jsonEqual`: same JS type, `Object.is`-free numeric `===` (so `1` equals `1.0`; `-0` equals `0`, numerically equal per §4.6), arrays pairwise, objects by own key set and values with order ignored. §4.6 leaves no room for loose equality.
+- **Errors.** One `JsonPatchError extends Error` for every failure, including invalid pointers (wrapped, message includes the operation index), in line with `JsonPointerError`'s single-catch approach. Operation members: `op` and `path` are checked with `typeof`; `value` presence uses `Object.hasOwn(op, "value")` so `null` is valid and `undefined`-valued members count as missing. Unknown extra members are ignored (§4).
+- **Large indexes** only fail range checks; no `Number` precision concerns matter because lengths are small.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Deep clone of the whole document per call is O(size)] → accepted for correctness and the no-mutation contract; the structural-sharing alternative adds complexity.
+- [Recursive clone/equality can overflow the stack on extremely deep documents] → inputs are parsed JSON, so depth is bounded by the parser; noted, not handled.
+- [`remove` of the root throws, where some libraries return `null`/`undefined`] → documented departure for an unspecified case.
