+# Proposal
+
+## Why
+
+Our services need to change JSON documents with standard patches rather than ad-hoc code. JSON Patch (RFC 6902) is that standard; it builds on the JSON Pointer support already in the library (RFC 6901).
+
+## What Changes
+
+- Add `applyPatch(document, patch)`, exported from `src/index.ts`: applies an array of RFC 6902 operations (`add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy`, `test`) in order and returns the resulting document as a new value. Neither `document` nor `patch` is mutated, and the result shares no mutable structure with either.
+- Add an exported `JsonPatchError` (extends `Error`), thrown for an invalid patch and for a failed operation, so callers can tell patch failures from programming errors.
+- Governing standards: RFC 6902 (§3 document structure, §4 operations, §5 error handling), with paths evaluated per RFC 6901 (§3–§4, `-` per RFC 6902 §4.1). Not breaking: purely additive.
+- Atomicity (RFC 6902 §5): if any operation fails, an error is thrown and no result is returned; the input is untouched.
+- Out of scope: parsing JSON text, the `application/json-patch+json` media type, JSON Merge Patch (RFC 7396), generating a patch by diffing, URI fragment pointers, non-JSON values (`undefined`, functions, cycles).
+
+## Capabilities
+
+### New Capabilities
+- `json-patch`: applying an RFC 6902 JSON Patch to a JSON document.
+
+### Modified Capabilities
+
+## Impact
+
+- New code in `src/index.ts` and tests under `test/` run by `npm test`; no runtime dependencies; `package.json` unchanged.
+- New public API surface (`applyPatch`, `JsonPatchError`). `resolvePointer` and `JsonPointerError` are unchanged.
